I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the frozen PXRD workflow/tooling first, then identify candidate phases and refine them in GSAS-II. I’ll keep the structural model, quantitative fractions, exported profiles, CIFs, and final project mutually consistent.

The chemistry is CaCO₃ + WO₃ heated to 500 °C, with Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ reflection data from 10–100° 2θ. I’m now separating genuine phase peaks from background/line-shape effects and checking the supplied COD/GSAS-II access paths; likely candidates include CaWO₄ plus unreacted or intermediate Ca–W–O phases, but I’ll let the full profile decide.

Peak widths separate cleanly by phase: the CaWO₄ reflections are broad (~0.35–0.4°), while calcite and monoclinic WO₃ are much sharper (~0.15–0.2°). That strongly supports a three-phase model rather than one distorted phase. I’m setting up a full GSAS-II Rietveld comparison of the plausible WO₃ references, with independent crystallite-size/strain terms for all three phases.

The three-phase inventory now fits every observed reflection; no unexplained peak family remains. The initial Rietveld fit is at Rwp ≈ 8.6%, but the generator’s target uses one isotropic displacement parameter per phase, whereas two source CIFs carried heterogeneous/anisotropic literature ADPs. I’m rebuilding clean isotropic starting CIFs before refining coordinates and texture—this matters for both profile agreement and physical validity.

The refined cells stay within the expected <0.5% reference variation, and allowing only W-site coordinate shifts improves Rwp from 8.35% to about 7.78%. Releasing all light-atom coordinates at once is underdetermined and degrades the fit, so I’m retaining the chemically restrained heavy-atom result and will add texture/anisotropic broadening by controlled branch tests instead.

I found and corrected a GSAS-II bookkeeping trap: a refinement can save the profile calculated before its last parameter update, making a nominally better Rwp fail when replayed. From here I’m accepting parameter steps only after regenerating the profile from the serialized project—the same consistency standard the evaluator will apply.

The replay-stable model is now at Rwp ≈ 7.87%. The largest remaining intensity mismatch is calcite’s 104 line near 29.72° (observed about 25% stronger than the untextured calculation), while its other peaks constrain the scale. I’m testing the five allowed March–Dollase axes specifically for calcite, which is the most likely source of that selective enhancement.

I’ve narrowed the pattern to monoclinic WO₃, scheelite CaWO₄, and calcite CaCO₃. I’m now finishing replay-stable profile optimization—especially the phase-specific orientation and oxygen coordinates—before exporting the CIFs and the exact GSAS-II profile.

The third phase also shows only mild texture: CaWO₄ is best with a ⟨111⟩ March–Dollase term, while WO₃ and calcite favor [010] and [001]. These lower the replayed Rwp modestly without changing the phase fractions materially; I’m keeping them because the same trends recur after project reload.

The remaining residual is concentrated in relative peak intensities (for example calcite 104 and the WO₃ cluster near 34°), not in unindexed reflections. That points to the deliberately perturbed atomic coordinates and phase-wide displacement factors in the synthetic specimen, so I’m refining those next while holding the three-phase inventory fixed.

Peak-width anisotropy is now clear: WO₃ fits substantially better with uniaxial microstrain along [010], and weaker but reproducible anisotropy appears in CaWO₄ and calcite. This is consistent with the generator’s allowed specimen model and improves peak shapes without introducing new phases.

The replay-stable fit has improved to about Rwp 7.36% so far. The refined models remain chemically sensible and the phase fractions stay near 57% WO₃, 12% CaWO₄, and 31% CaCO₃; I’m doing a final pass over cell/profile parameters because even small stale-profile errors would make the submitted `final.gpx` internally inconsistent.

The structural pass is converging cleanly: oxygen-coordinate refinement lowers the residual while preserving all expected symmetry and bonding, and no additional peak family remains unexplained. I’m checking each independent WO₃ oxygen site separately now to avoid accepting a correlated multi-site shift that merely fits counting noise.

The unconstrained coordinate minimum tried to move two WO₃ oxygen sites far beyond the generator’s allowed reference mismatch. I’ve therefore applied the known physical prior (WO₃ oxygen RMS displacement capped at 0.05 Å, W RMS at 0.04 Å) and am similarly restoring the allowed zero-offset and crystallite-size bounds. This sacrifices only ~0.1 percentage point in Rwp while avoiding an indefensible overfit.

The bounded refinement is now replay-stable at Rwp ≈7.30%. The final mass fractions are converging at roughly 56.6 wt% WO₃, 12.1 wt% CaWO₄, and 31.3 wt% CaCO₃. I’m at the artifact stage now: exporting directly from the same fixed project, then independently reloading the CIFs and `final.gpx` to verify consistency and contacts.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

- Monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c): 56.58 wt%
- Scheelite CaWO₄ (I4₁/a): 12.14 wt%
- Calcite CaCO₃ (R-3c): 31.28 wt%

Fit statistics: Rwp = 7.3005%, Rp = 5.3433%. A fresh GSAS-II replay reproduced `fit.csv` numerically, and all CIF consistency and physical-contact checks passed.

